The bundled Python backend is built into this directory by
`node scripts/build-backend-runtime.mjs`, and everything it writes here is
git-ignored — it is roughly a gigabyte of interpreter and site-packages.

This placeholder is committed so the `extraResources` entry in
electron-builder.json always has a directory to copy. Without it, a developer who
has never built the runtime gets a packaging error about a missing directory, on a
build that had no intention of shipping a backend. The build script clears this
directory and then writes the placeholder back, so it survives a build too.

An app packaged with only this file present still starts: `bundledRuntimeIn`
(apps/desktop/src/backend.ts) checks for `backend/app.py` and the interpreter, not
for the directory, so an incomplete runtime reports "no backend found" rather than
spawning something that cannot import itself.
